meta {
  name: Impersonate User
  type: http
  seq: 9
}

post {
  url: {{base_url}}/users/{{user_id}}/impersonate
  body: none
  auth: inherit
}

params:path {
  user_id: {{user_id}}
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
  res.body.access_token: isDefined
}

docs {
  # Impersonate User

  Signs the caller in as another user without that user's password, for
  support and debugging. Requires permission `user_impersonate`.

  The returned access token opens **no login session**: it is not stored
  server-side, cannot be revoked before it expires, and carries no refresh
  token. It simply dies after `IMPERSONATION_TOKEN_EXPIRE_MINUTES` (30 by
  default), which is why that window is kept short. Use it as a normal
  `Authorization: Bearer` token; `/auth/me` then answers as the target user
  with `is_impersonating: true`, while `/auth/logout` and the session routes
  refuse it because there is no session behind it.

  Refused with `403` when the target is the caller themselves, or when the
  target also holds `user_impersonate` — impersonating another privileged
  account would be an escalation path.

  ## Input (path)
  | Param | Type | Required |
  |-------|------|----------|
  | user_id | uuid | yes |

  ## Output `200`
  ```json
  { "access_token": "eyJhbGciOi...", "token_type": "bearer" }
  ```
}
